🚀 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟰/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗖𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀 & 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗚𝗮𝗹𝗮𝘅𝘆

Continuing my hashtag#30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I explored Ansible Collections and Ansible Galaxy — two important concepts for building reusable and maintainable automation.

📦 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗮𝗿𝗲 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗖𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀?

Collections are a distribution format for Ansible content.

They can contain:

✔️ Modules
✔️ Plugins
✔️ Roles
✔️ Playbooks
✔️ Module utilities

Instead of having everything inside one large project, collections help organize and distribute reusable automation.

🔍 𝗙𝗶𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗖𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀

Ansible Galaxy provides a large ecosystem of community and vendor-supported content.

You can search for collections using:

ansible-galaxy collection search

📥 𝗜𝗻𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗹𝗹 𝗮 𝗖𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

For example:

ansible-galaxy collection install community.general

Then modules from that collection can be used in your playbooks.

Example:

- name: Manage a package
 community.general.snap:
 name: docker
 state: present

🧩 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗚𝗮𝗹𝗮𝘅𝘆

Ansible Galaxy is a hub/ecosystem where you can discover, install, and share Ansible content, including roles and collections.

Common commands:

ansible-galaxy role search nginx
ansible-galaxy role install username.role_name
ansible-galaxy collection install community.general

💡 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗖𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀 𝗠𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿

Imagine your team repeatedly needs automation for:

☁️ AWS
🐳 Docker
☸️ Kubernetes
🔧 Linux
🗄️ Databases

Instead of reinventing automation from scratch, you can leverage existing collections and focus on your application requirements.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Collections = packaged Ansible content

Galaxy = ecosystem for discovering and sharing Ansible content

Together, they make Ansible automation more modular, reusable, and scalable. 🚀

